add("eu-digital-fairness-act-loot-boxes-gambling-influencer-marketing",
"EU Digital Fairness Act: Loot Boxes and Gambling Ads","Regulatory",
"The Commission's draft DFA would bar paid loot boxes for minors and target gambling influencer posts. Here is what operators should prepare for.",
["malta","sweden","denmark","netherlands","germany"],["bird-and-bird","cms","wh-partners","mannheimer-swartling","kalff-katz-and-franssen"],"2026-11-13T07:30:00Z",[
"The European Commission has published its long-awaited draft Digital Fairness Act, a regulation that would replace the 2005 Unfair Commercial Practices Directive with directly applicable EU-wide rules on digital commercial practices. For the gambling and gaming sectors, two parts of the draft matter most: a new regime for paid 'variable reward systems', which captures loot boxes and similar mechanics, and a sharper focus on influencer marketing of products the Commission regards as unsuitable for children, a category in which betting and lotteries are expressly named.",
"Article 15 of the draft is the core loot box provision. It applies to virtual items offering random rewards or variable odds that are bought with money or with paid virtual currency, and the recitals give loot boxes, wheels of fortune and collectible card packs as examples. Traders would have to disclose, clearly and prominently and before purchase, the types of items available and the probability of obtaining each one. They would also have to give every consumer, not only minors, accessible and effective tools to switch these features off. The draft is deliberately technology-neutral and does not prescribe how that switch must be built.",
"The most consequential rule concerns children. Paid variable reward systems could not be offered in products aimed at minors, or where the trader knows with reasonable certainty that minors use the product. The restriction is narrower than some headlines suggest: it targets randomised rewards purchased wholly or partly with money or paid virtual currency, not every random reward in a game. Even so, the 'reasonable certainty' knowledge standard will be contested. Studios with mixed-age audiences will need defensible age-assurance evidence, and the line between a game 'aimed at' children and one merely popular with them will become a litigation question in member states.",
"For licensed gambling operators, the more immediate exposure lies in Recital 55, which names betting, lotteries, speculative instruments and other gambling products as potentially harmful or unsuitable, particularly for children, and ties that concern to influencer marketing. The Commission worries that children may treat influencers as friends, fail to recognise commercial recommendations, and be exposed to posts that obscure their commercial purpose. The draft builds on existing sectoral advertising restrictions rather than creating a standalone gambling ad ban, but it would give consumer protection authorities a harmonised basis to act against affiliate and influencer content that already sits uneasily with national gambling advertising codes.",
"The interaction with national gambling law is where specialist advice will be needed. Malta, Sweden, Denmark, the Netherlands and Germany already restrict untargeted gambling advertising and, in several cases, influencer and role-model marketing. A directly applicable regulation layered on top of those regimes raises questions of enforcement competence: whether the gambling regulator, the consumer authority or both will act, and how sanctions will be coordinated. Operators licensed in one member state but marketing across borders through creators based in another should expect the cross-border cooperation machinery of EU consumer law to become a real enforcement channel.",
"There is also a definitional fight ahead. Industry voices have already argued that loot boxes are not equivalent to regulated gambling because they generally cannot be cashed out, and the draft does not attempt to classify them as gambling. That is important for operators: it means the DFA is unlikely to pull games into gambling licensing regimes, but equally it does not preclude national regulators from doing so where items can be traded or converted into value. Research cited in the debate, including Swedish data linking childhood loot box purchases to later risky gambling, and Denmark's public campaign on gambling-like features in children's games, show that regulators are building an evidential record.",
"The draft must now be negotiated by the European Parliament and the Council, and if adopted in its current form would apply 12 months after entry into force. That gives businesses time, but not much. We recommend that gambling operators audit influencer and affiliate agreements for disclosure, age-targeting and content-approval controls; that game publishers and social casino providers map every paid randomised mechanic against Article 15; and that both sectors engage with trade associations during the legislative process, where the scope of the minors restriction and the knowledge standard are most likely to move."])

add("mexico-lfpiorpi-acuerdo-115-2026-aml-rules-gambling-operators-30-november",
"Mexico's New AML Rules Hit Gambling Permit Holders","Compliance",
"Acuerdo 115/2026 takes effect on 30 November, bringing risk-based AML duties, 10-year retention and tougher fines to Mexico's gaming sector.",
["mexico","united-states"],["greenberg-traurig-llp","holland-and-knight-llp","akerman-llp","duane-morris-llp"],"2026-11-13T08:45:00Z",[
"Mexico's reformed anti-money laundering framework reaches its most important milestone on 30 November 2026, when the amended General Rules under the Federal Law for the Prevention and Identification of Operations with Resources of Illicit Origin (LFPIORPI) take effect. The rules, published in the Diario Oficial de la Federación on 7 August 2026 as Acuerdo 115/2026, complete a reform sequence that began with amendments to the statute in July 2025 and to its implementing regulation in March 2026. For casinos, sportsbooks, online operators and raffle organisers holding permits from the Secretaría de Gobernación, the changes convert AML compliance from a reporting exercise into a documented, risk-based programme.",
"Gambling, contests and raffles remain a 'vulnerable activity' under Article 17 of the LFPIORPI, and the reform makes clear that compliance with the Federal Law on Games and Raffles does not displace AML obligations. Permit holders must be registered on the SAT's anti-money laundering portal under the gambling activity, screen customers against the UIF's lists and the UN Security Council consolidated list, and file notices by the 17th of the month following a reportable transaction. A 24-hour notice is required where there are indications that funds are illicit or where a customer matches a UIF list, even if the transaction did not proceed.",
"The quantitative thresholds remain expressed in UMA, Mexico's unit of measurement. As reported by compliance practitioners, customer identification is triggered at 325 UMA, a notice at 645 UMA, and cash may not be used to buy tickets or pay prizes at 3,210 UMA or above; such prizes must be paid by transfer or nominative cheque. Linked transactions with the same customer within 24 hours are aggregated for identification, and like transactions are accumulated over up to six months for notice purposes. Operators running digital platforms should note that the aggregation rules expressly cover online activity, which makes wallet-level monitoring rather than venue-level monitoring the appropriate design.",
"The structural changes are what will demand most work. Each obligated entity must design and document its own risk-assessment methodology, using at least 12 months of historical data and recording the mitigants it relies on; that methodology and an updated internal policies manual must be in place by 1 March 2027. The compliance representative must hold UIF certification, valid for five years. Automated monitoring mechanisms are due by 1 June 2027, the first mandatory annual training cycle runs through 2027, and the first audit period falls in 2028, with independent external audit expected for higher-risk entities. Beneficial ownership identification continues to apply the more-than-25 per cent or effective-control test.",
"Record retention doubles. Since the 2025 statutory reform, records must be kept for ten years rather than five, at the domicile registered with Hacienda. For online operators whose platforms are hosted or managed by foreign B2B providers, that requirement should prompt a review of data-access and exit clauses in supplier contracts: a permit holder cannot demonstrate compliance if its transaction history sits with a platform provider it no longer controls.",
"The sanctions regime is significant. Fines for failing to file a notice, or for accepting or paying cash above the limit, can reach between 10,000 and 65,000 UMA or 10 to 100 per cent of the amount involved, whichever is greater, and repeated serious breaches can be reported to the Secretaría de Gobernación with a view to permit revocation under the gaming law. In a market where SEGOB has already been suspending permitted domains and where wider reform of the 1947 gaming law remains stalled, AML failures have become one of the most direct routes by which the authorities can put a permit at risk.",
"Our practical recommendation is to treat 30 November as the start of a 15-month programme rather than a single deadline. Permit holders should confirm portal registration and compliance-officer designation now, begin assembling the 12 months of data their risk methodology will need, and map which obligations their platform providers must support technically. Foreign operators and suppliers working with Mexican permit holders should expect revised contractual demands around data retention, monitoring tools and audit cooperation in the coming months."])

add("france-anj-decision-2026-158-data-vault-technical-requirements-30-november",
"France ANJ Data Vault Rules Take Effect 30 November","Licensing",
"ANJ Decision 2026-158 replaces France's data archiving specification, reshaping how licensed operators capture and expose player data.",
[],["bird-and-bird","cms","fieldfisher","taylor-wessing","pinsent-masons-llp"],"2026-11-13T10:00:00Z",[
"On 30 November 2026, France's Autorité Nationale des Jeux will bring into force Decision 2026-158, a 210-page technical specification governing how licensed online operators make gaming data available to the regulator. Adopted by the ANJ's college on 21 July 2026 and published on 24 July, the decision repeals Decision 2021-212 of September 2021 and Decision 2023-187 of July 2023 and applies to data deposited from its entry-into-force date. It has attracted far less commentary than the regulator's sanctions decisions, but for operators it may prove more consequential, because it governs the infrastructure through which the ANJ supervises them every day.",
"The legal foundation is Articles 31 and 38 of the Law of 12 May 2010. Article 31 requires operators to archive in real time, on a storage medium located in mainland France, the data relating to players, accounts and game events, with all exchanges between the player and the operator passing through that archive. Article 38 underpins the ANJ's permanent supervision and defines the categories of data it can access. Holding a French licence therefore means operating a regulator-approved archiving system, and changes to its technical specification are in substance changes to licence conditions.",
"The decision preserves the familiar architecture of a capture module, the 'capteur', which writes traces of events as they occur, and a vault, the 'coffre-fort', which stores them. Traces are encrypted with the ANJ's public key so that only ANJ agents can decrypt the stored data. The capture module authenticates to the vault using an X.509v3 certificate, transfers rely on mutually authenticated TLS, records are signed in XAdES-T format with RFC 3161 timestamps, and operator authentication keys must be at least 2048-bit RSA. Operators must also implement transport- and application-level protections against denial-of-service attacks.",
"The data model itself is detailed. Each record is an XML structure combining validation data and game data, encoded in UTF-8 with UTC timestamps synchronised to within one second. Event headers carry, among other fields, the operator identifier, a sequential event identifier, the player identifier and hash, IP address and session identifier. Validation data includes a hash of the preceding validation record, creating a chained, tamper-evident log, and where events are batched, their identifiers must be consecutive. Any gap or break in the chain is therefore visible to the regulator.",
"Access rights are the part of the decision with the clearest supervisory implications. Remote access must cover at least the last 12 months of activity on a rolling basis, and the ANJ can extract data remotely or on site, including onto secured removable media, using tools that must run on Windows and Linux and function offline. Four roles are defined: depositor, reader, technical or operational administrator, and functional administrator. In practice, this means the regulator can test an operator's player-protection and AML claims against raw event data rather than relying on what the operator reports.",
"That is the strategic point. The ANJ has made clear through recent sanctions, including penalties for archiving and data-availability failures and for inadequate detection of at-risk players, that it expects its findings to rest on evidence. A modernised data specification with richer, chained event data makes that evidence easier to obtain. Gaps in the vault are no longer a technical irritant; they can become the factual basis of a sanctions referral, and an inability to reconstruct a player's journey may be treated as a compliance failure in itself.",
"With little more than two weeks remaining, operators should confirm that their capture and vault implementations have been updated and tested against the new event specifications, that certificate and key management meets the new parameters, and that platform suppliers have delivered the required changes. Contracts with B2B providers should allocate responsibility for compliance with the specification and for cooperation with ANJ extractions. Operators who discover gaps should consider engaging with the regulator before 30 November rather than after a failed extraction."])

add("massachusetts-gaming-commission-prediction-market-complaints-congress-testimony",
"Massachusetts: Prediction Market Complaints It Can't Fix","Regulatory",
"The MGC says residents seek help with prediction market disputes it cannot resolve, as its chair urges Congress to protect state authority.",
["united-states"],["ifrah-law-pllc","covington-and-burling-llp","fox-rothschild-llp","greenberg-traurig-llp","zwillgen-pllc"],"2026-11-13T11:20:00Z",[
"The Massachusetts Gaming Commission has disclosed that it is receiving dozens of requests from residents asking it to intervene in disputes with prediction market platforms, and that it has no power to help. The MGC's response, according to a spokesperson, is to refer those consumers to the Commodity Futures Trading Commission. The disclosure came the same week that MGC Chair Jordan Maynard told a House subcommittee hearing on sports gambling on college campuses, on 11 November 2026, that the parallel existence of state-licensed sportsbooks and federally supervised prediction markets has left consumers with what he called two sets of standards.",
"The complaints illustrate a consumer protection gap that has received less attention than the jurisdictional litigation. A Massachusetts sports bettor using a licensed sportsbook can escalate a dispute to the MGC, which has enforcement tools and a record of using them: Maynard's written testimony cited monetary penalties in 39 enforcement actions, more than $1 million collected from licensed operators, and nearly $10 million returned to patrons for operator errors. A user of a sports event contract on a prediction market has none of those state remedies. The platforms concerned have never obtained a Massachusetts sports wagering licence.",
"Maynard's testimony focused on substantive differences. Massachusetts requires sports bettors to be 21, imposes advertising restrictions and prohibits proposition bets on individual college athletes. Prediction markets generally permit users aged 18 and over and, he said, list contracts on individual college athletes. From a consumer's perspective, he argued, a sports event contract can be almost indistinguishable from a licensed bet. He urged Congress to preserve state and tribal authority over sports betting and to enact a national prohibition on college player proposition bets.",
"Legally, the MGC's position reflects the core dispute in the prediction market cases now running through federal and state courts: whether sports event contracts listed on CFTC-designated contract markets are swaps within the CFTC's exclusive jurisdiction under the Commodity Exchange Act, or wagers that remain subject to state gaming law. Courts have reached different conclusions, and the CFTC's recent guidance discouraging exchanges from geofencing state users has intensified the conflict. The consumer-complaint data adds a policy dimension: if states cannot regulate these products, the question becomes whether the federal framework provides equivalent dispute resolution, age verification and integrity protections.",
"For prediction market operators, the testimony is a warning that the political debate is moving from jurisdiction to standards. Even a definitive federal win on pre-emption may not end pressure for Congress or the CFTC to impose sportsbook-style protections, including age limits, restrictions on collegiate contracts, advertising rules and complaint-handling obligations. Platforms that build those protections voluntarily will be better placed in rulemaking and in any litigation where a court weighs public interest factors on injunctive relief.",
"For licensed sportsbooks, the episode reinforces the regulatory asymmetry they have complained about. They bear licensing fees, high state tax rates, responsible gaming mandates and enforcement exposure while competing with products that reach younger customers and offer markets they cannot. Licensed operators considering partnerships with prediction market exchanges, or launching their own event contract products in other states, should weigh the reputational and licensing risk carefully: state regulators have made clear that conduct elsewhere can bear on suitability in their jurisdiction.",
"The practical takeaway for all market participants is that consumer complaint data is now part of the evidentiary record. State regulators are documenting harm and confusion and presenting it to Congress, and plaintiffs' lawyers will notice. Operators on both sides of the divide should review their dispute-resolution processes, age-assurance controls and college sports offerings now, rather than waiting for a federal statute or court ruling to define the minimum standard."])

add("iagr-check-before-you-play-illegal-gambling-awareness-week-canada",
"IAGR Licence Checker Targets Illegal Gambling in Canada","Enforcement",
"IAGR launched CheckBeforeYouPlay.org during Illegal Gambling Awareness Week, with Ontario, BC and Manitoba among covered regulators.",
["canada","united-kingdom"],["dickinson-wright-pllc","fox-rothschild-llp","harris-hagan","joelson-llp"],"2026-11-13T12:40:00Z",[
"The International Association of Gaming Regulators has launched a free licence verification tool, CheckBeforeYouPlay.org, allowing consumers to enter a gambling site's web address and see licensing information for participating IAGR member jurisdictions. In Canada, the covered regulators include the Alcohol and Gaming Commission of Ontario, British Columbia's Gaming Policy and Enforcement Branch and Manitoba's Liquor, Gaming and Cannabis Authority. The tool, developed by Paris-based regulatory technology company Sharp Vision, was released as part of Illegal Gambling Awareness Week, held from 9 to 13 November 2026 and described as the first internationally coordinated campaign on the risks of unlicensed online gambling.",
"IAGR chief executive Kevin Mullally framed the problem as one of perception, observing that people often mistake visibility for trust. That is a pointed remark in an environment where unlicensed sites advertise through search, social media and affiliates and can look indistinguishable from licensed brands. IAGR has highlighted the practical harms consumers face on such sites: unpaid winnings, limited routes to resolve disputes and the misuse of personal financial information. The AGCO and iGaming Ontario both issued statements recognising the week.",
"Legally, a licence checker is a modest tool, but its significance lies in what it signals about regulatory cooperation. A shared, URL-based registry across jurisdictions creates an authoritative reference point that payment providers, advertising platforms, search engines and app stores can use. Regulators across Europe and elsewhere have increasingly shifted their enforcement focus from blocking websites to disrupting the infrastructure around illegal operators, including payments, marketing and suppliers, as the UK Gambling Commission's recent statements on targeting criminal funds illustrate. A common licence registry makes it harder for intermediaries to claim they could not tell whether a site was authorised.",
"For licensed operators in Ontario, the timing is helpful. The province had 48 licensed operators and 83 igaming websites as of 12 November, and an Ipsos study released by iGaming Ontario in May found that 91.1 per cent of online gamblers played on regulated sites, up from 83.7 per cent the previous year. September data showed a record 1.413 million active player accounts and total revenue of CA$416.3 million. Those figures support the argument that a regulated model can channel most play, but the residual grey and black market remains material, particularly where offshore sites continue to target Canadian players outside Ontario's regime.",
"The tool also matters for provinces still running monopoly or hybrid models. Outside Ontario and the recently opened Alberta market, many Canadian players remain within reach of offshore sites that hold licences elsewhere but not provincially. A cross-border checker that shows whether a site is licensed in the player's own province reframes the consumer message from 'is this site licensed somewhere' to 'is this site licensed here', which is the legally relevant question under the Criminal Code's provincial-control model.",
"Operators and suppliers should take two practical points from the campaign. First, licensed brands should ensure their domains, including mirror and regional domains, are correctly reflected in regulators' public registers, because consumers and intermediaries will increasingly rely on these tools; an omitted domain risks being treated as suspect. Second, B2B suppliers and affiliates serving Canadian traffic should expect closer scrutiny of where their content and games appear. Regulators are building the data infrastructure needed to identify unlicensed sites at scale, and the next step is likely to be pressure on the businesses that supply them.",
"Illegal Gambling Awareness Week will be judged by whether it produces sustained cooperation rather than a single burst of publicity. If IAGR expands the checker to more jurisdictions and secures uptake by payment and advertising platforms, it could become a meaningful enforcement asset. Operators that position themselves as visible partners in that effort, rather than passive beneficiaries, will strengthen their standing with regulators during licence renewals and policy consultations."])

